text:
Q: Minimum no. of comparisons to find median of 3 numbers A: <p>The minimum number of comparisons needed is three in the general case. This is a well known fact in sorting theory that can be easily proven by noting that there are 3!=6 possible input orderings which corresponds to lg(6)~2.585 unknown bits of information, and given that one comparison can only resolve no more than one bit of information, the number N of comparisons needed must be N &gt;= ceil(lg(6)) = 3 .</p>
<p>Some of the answers presented seemingly try to avoid this fact by calling min/max functions instead of doing comparisons, but this does little more than hide the CMP behind a function call, given that in order to find the min/max of a pair of values the compiler ends up emitting a CMP instruction followed by a conditional load / move. Incidentally, a language call to a 3-input min/max is nothing more than TWO consecutive calls to their 2-input counterparts.</p>
<p>Other answers attempt to avoid comparisons by substituting them with subtractions (and/or additions), which is a similarly flawed path considering the critical path in the logic circuit for sub -that is, the computation of the resulting sign- is essentially a CMP. Or, in other words, a CMP is a SUB if we just keep the sign bit flag and discard the result rather than storing it back to a register.</p>
<p>In short, both approaches are basically performing an equivalent (or larger!) number of comparisons behind the scenes.</p>
<p>Coming back to the 3 comparison solution, the safest and most efficient bet is to translate any (of the six equivalent) 3-input minimal sorting networks into a function and discard any values other than the median as soon as we no longer need them. This is exemplified in the following code:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int median (int a, int b, int c) {
    if (b&gt;a) swap(a,b); // we have to keep a &lt;- max(a,b) for step 3
    b = max (b,c); // equivalent to:  b = (b&gt;c)? b : c;
    b = min (a,b); // equivalent to:  b = (a&lt;b)? a : b;
    return b;
}
</code></pre>
<p>which, as explained before, translates into three machine-code comparisons.</p>
<p>Now, that being said, there are specific cases where one <em>can</em> avoid using comparisons, namely by avoiding comparison sorts entirely. This can be achieved with alternative integer sorting techniques under the common word-RAM model of computation, as long as there are additional constraints on the input that allow it and are known in advance.</p>
<p>If, for instance, the integers happen either be in the 0 to 2^6~2^9 range (depending on architecture and other specific assumptions) we can sort them using ZERO comparisons using count sort / bucket sort <em>implicitly</em> and leveraging hardware bit-level parallelism with a biset as wide as a native register, a SIMD register, or a cache line.</p>
<p>More generally, this technique can be used when we know the inputs can only differ by that much (i.e. the least significant 6~9 bits) or -even more generally- if we can figure out a bit mask that removes redundant or &quot;inactive&quot; bits from the integers and select a subset of 6-9 of them which is sufficient to correctly sort the inputs.</p>
<p>In this case, we can populate the bitset with three (or more) entries at a cost of O(1) operations per value and extract the median with a select() operation, which <em>may</em> also be possible in O(1) time depending on the instruction set and implementation details.</p>
<p>Needless to say, this is a complete overkill in the specific case of finding the median of three integers, but it could possibly speed-up the computation of longer, yet still relatively small sets of integers, and/or algorithms that require multiple select() operations on the same integer set, provided the conditions discussed earlier can be guaranteed. For instance, the median-of-medians algorithm which is also commonly used to find a pivot in quicksort requires finding the medians of integer subsets of size 5, which requires no less than 7 comparisons assuming nonbranching (sequential) code</p>
<p>The following is an outline for a routine using the bitset method, assuming x86-64 BMI2 (bit manipulation) extensions are available:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>// assumes the most significant 32-6 bits of a,b and c are identical
uint32_t median_bound (uint32_t a, uint32_t b, uint32_t c) {

    uint32_t hi = a &amp; ~(0x3Ful); // save high 32-6 bits of a 

    // create a 64-element bitset and set three keys corresponding to the low 6 bits of a,b,c
    uint64_t set;
    set = 1ull &lt;&lt; (a &amp; 0x3F) 
        | 1ull &lt;&lt; (b &amp; 0x3F)
        | 1ull &lt;&lt; (c &amp; 0x3F);

    // extract the middle value of the bitset in O(1) time
    uint64_t mbit = _pdep_u64 ( 0b00000010, set); // selects the middle bit and zeroes the rest
    uint32_t lo = __builtin_ctzll ( mbit ) ;  // find the index of the set bit by counting trailing zeroes

    uint32_t median =  hi|lo;
    return median;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Besides the previously discussed restrictions, this routine will not correctly handle the special case of repeated values in general, although there are simple ways to extend it that will also handle such cases.</p>
<p>Despite its shortcomings it should perform relatively well given the low cost and delay of the CPU instructions used; bit shifts and word boolean operations not only have a latency of  a single CPU cycle, but are also computed in parallel several at a time on modern CPUs, bit population count (ctzll) instructions has a lower throughput of 1 instruction/cycle on AMD Zen cores, but executes in a single cycle nevertheless,  and parallel bit deposit has a higher latency of 3 cycles on a Zen 2+ core, but can overlap execution and get the same 1 instruction/cycle throughput. Another big advantage is that the code contains no conditional branches, which means its runtime behavior is sequential and therefore perfectly predictable, and also straightforward to vectorize.</p>
<p>This routine can be easily adapted to the most general case previously discussed with the use of the parallel bit extract BMI2 instruction on the inputs with a bitmask known in advance.</p>
<p>A general technique to find such a bitmask potentially applicable to many cases is the so-called &quot;integer sketching&quot;, as outlined in the wiki article for Fusion tree:</p>
<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fusion_tree" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fusion_tree</a></p>
